any also develops and sells computerized systems that distribute, display, store, and retrieve digital images; picture archiving communications systems and related services; and develops and deploys AI suites to enhance radiologist interpretations of breast, lung, and prostate images, as well as solutions for prostate cancer screening. In addition, it develops and delivers AI-powered health informatics solutions to drive quality, efficiency, and outcomes in imaging and radiology; informatics designed for outpatient radiology; and DeepHealth OS, a cloud-native operating system that helps in the operations of the radiology service. RadNet, Inc. was founded in 1981 and is headquartered in Los Angeles, California.","ceo_bio":"Dr. Howard G. Berger M.D. | Chairman, President & CEO | Age 79 | Compensation: $3.1M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 12K people. Revenue per employee of $170K ranks in the bottom quartile among Healthcare peers, with revenue growing 9%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $7K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $5K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 18\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (7/10).","financial_summary":["RadNet, Inc. is a mid-cap healthcare business with a $6.0B market cap on $2.0B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +13% over the past three years and +10% annually across the full decade, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at 7% against a 9% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The business throws off $86M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 4 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 1.5\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and thin 0.9\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 9.9% of the shares, and profit capture is only 0.3\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 122% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":1.2241079017965162},{"text":"Piotroski score 3/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Elevated short interest (13.3% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting the other way","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Significant insider selling (net $8.4M)","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","dollars":-8356012.19},{"text":"Patent cliffs, drug-pricing reform, and reimbursement pressure \u2014 a single regulatory change can reset the pricing power that justifies the multiple.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"GLP-1 disruption across sub-sectors \u2014 weight-loss drugs are reshaping demand for orthopaedics, cardiovascular devices, and sleep-apnea treatments in ways not yet priced in.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Healthcare sector showing strong relative momentum, outperforming the market by 13% over 3 months","Healthcare ETF in a sustained uptrend above its long-term average","Str